  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nMonday, a closed mid-level southern-stream trough will move into the \nregion through the day before ejecting northeastward during the \nafternoon and evening. Ahead of this system, warm air advection will \nincrease as southerly to southwesterly flow becomes established in \nthe 700-850 mb layer. Temperatures at 850 mb will warm into the 25 \nto 30C range, which is quite warm for this time of year. At the \nsurface, low pressure will deepen across North Dakota, with a \nsecondary low developing in the lee of the Rockies. This will \nmaintain southerly flow across the area ahead of an approaching cold \nfront. Ahead of the front, deep boundary-layer mixing beneath the \nwarm air aloft should allow temperatures to surge into the middle \n80s to lower 90s. Winds will remain somewhat gusty, though minimum \nrelative humidity values should remain above 30 percent, keeping \noverall fire weather concerns low. In terms of precipitation, \nuncertainty remains regarding how far southeast the front progresses \nbefore showers and thunderstorms develop along it. Current model \nguidance generally favors the greatest convective coverage just \nsoutheast of the forecast area, though some showers and \nthunderstorms remain possible across eastern portions of the area \nMonday afternoon and evening depending on the eventual position and \ntiming of the front. The overall severe weather threat appears low \nat this time, though sufficient instability and deep-layer shear \ncould support a few strong thunderstorms. Overnight lows will range \nfrom the middle 50s to lower 60s.\n\nTuesday, the cold front will move south of the area and become \nnearly stationary across Kansas, leaving the forecast area firmly \nwithin the cooler air mass. Strengthening mid-level cold air \nadvection and lingering cloud cover should hold afternoon highs in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. A baroclinic zone will remain \nestablished along the stalled boundary, with strengthening warm air \nadvection and isentropic ascent supporting an overrunning \nprecipitation regime. The greatest precipitation chances should \nremain south of the forecast area across Kansas, though some \nprecipitation could extend into southern portions of the area \ndepending on the eventual position of the boundary. Tuesday night \nlows will range from the middle 40s north to the middle 50s south.\n\nWednesday through the weekend, the pattern will remain active as \nmultiple weak disturbances move through the region while the mid-\nlevel baroclinic zone and surface boundary remain near or just south \nof the area. This setup will support periodic opportunities for \nprecipitation, though the greatest chances currently appear focused \nfrom northern Kansas into southern Nebraska. The exact position of \nthe stationary boundary will play an important role in determining \nhow far north the more persistent precipitation can develop. By late \nweek, model guidance continues to suggest a stronger system moving \ninto the region. This could provide the best opportunity for more \nwidespread precipitation during the extended period and may be \naccompanied by another stronger cold frontal passage. Confidence in \nthe exact timing and evolution remains limited at this range. Highs \nthrough much of the period should remain in the upper 60s to lower \n70s, with overnight lows generally in the lower to middle 50s. \nOverall, a more fall-like pattern is expected through the long-term \nperiod, characterized by cooler temperatures and periodic rainfall \nchances.",
